    "type": "mc",
    "prompt": "If $ABCD$ is a square, $\\angle EBF=\\angle CBF$, $AE=2$ and $CF=3$, then the length of $EB$ is",
    "image": "qimages/samf/senior-2016-r1/q20.webp",
    "choices": [
      {
        "key": "A",
        "text": "$\\sqrt{13}$"
      },
      {
        "key": "B",
        "text": "5"
      },
      {
        "key": "C",
        "text": "6"
      },
      {
        "key": "D",
        "text": "$\\sqrt{29}$"
      },
      {
        "key": "E",
        "text": "$4\\sqrt3$"
      }
    ],
    "answer": "B",
    "acceptedAnswers": null,
    "answerUnit": null,
    "solutions": [
      {
        "title": "Trigonometry",
        "body": "Let $\\angle CBF=\\angle EBF=\\theta$, $x$ the side of the square. From $\\triangle BCF$: $\\tan\\theta=3/x$. Since $AD\\parallel BC$, $\\angle AEB=\\angle EBC=2\\theta$, and in $\\triangle BAE$, $\\tan2\\theta=x/2$. With $\\tan2\\theta=\\frac{2t}{1-t^2}$, $t=3/x$: $\\frac x2=\\frac{6x}{x^2-9}$, so $x^2-9=12$, $x^2=21$ and $EB^2=x^2+4=25$, $EB=5$.\n\n**Answer: (B) 5**"
      },
      {
        "title": "Rotation",
        "body": "Rotate $\\triangle BAE$ by $90^\\circ$ about $B$ so that $A$ goes to $C$ and $E$ to $E'$ on line $DC$ extended beyond $C$, with $CE'=AE=2$ and $BE'=BE$. Now $\\angle E'BF=\\angle E'BC+\\angle CBF=(90^\\circ-2\\theta)+\\theta=90^\\circ-\\theta$, and $\\angle BFE'=\\angle BFC=90^\\circ-\\theta$, so $E'B=E'F=2+3=5$. Hence $EB=5$.\n\n**Answer: (B) 5**"
      }
    ],
    "topics": [
      "geometry",
      "trigonometry"
    ],
